<div class="Section1">

<h1>SERS Sensor Development Roadmap — Sepsis Biomarker Platform (NAM / LTA / LPS)</h1>
<p class="subtitle"><strong>Sukesh P (M24IM1007)</strong> &middot; TCS-funded project, IIT Jodhpur &amp; AIIMS Jodhpur &middot; Project No. R/S/TCS/AA/20250112</p>
<p class="subtitle">Engineering roadmap built strictly from your project documents (August 2026 experimental work as primary evidence, prior months' DFT/reference work as supporting evidence).</p>

<div class="key"><strong>How to read this document.</strong> Every claim is tagged: <span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> = completed work with documented evidence &middot; <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span> = protocol already written, not yet executed &middot; <span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED]</span> = new engineering suggestion in this roadmap, not yet in any project document &middot; <span class="tag-assume">[ASSUMPTION]</span> = inference/reasoning, not a documented fact. Where a document doesn't say something, this roadmap says so explicitly rather than filling the gap.</div>

<hr>

<h2>PART 1 — Define the Final Sensor</h2>

<h3>What sensing element and substrate are we actually working with?</h3>
<p><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> The sensing element demonstrated to date is a chemically-fabricated <strong>silver-on-silicon (Ag/black-Si) SERS-active surface</strong>, made by one-step metal-assisted chemical etching (MACE): a silicon piece is immersed in a single bath containing AgNO&#8323; and HF, so silver is reduced and deposited <em>at the same time</em> that the HF etches the silicon into a nanostructured ("black") surface. This is the only substrate architecture that has actually produced data (0.170&nbsp;g AgNO&#8323; + 8.6&nbsp;mL HF in 50&nbsp;mL DI, etch times 2/5/10&nbsp;min tested).</p>

<h3>Role of Ag</h3>
<p><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> Silver nanoparticles/nanostructures deposited on the silicon provide the plasmonic "hot spots" responsible for SERS enhancement &mdash; confirmed functionally by the R6G benchmark (SNR up to ~450 at the best condition). <span class="tag-assume">[ASSUMPTION]</span> The exact particle size/gap distribution has never been measured (no FESEM/EDS on this batch), so "why" the enhancement works at the nanostructure level is inferred from literature (Kochylas et al. 2021 for one-step Ag/HF MACE), not measured on this project's own substrates.</p>

<h3>Role of Black Silicon</h3>
<p><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> The HF etch simultaneously roughens/nanostructures the silicon surface ("black silicon"), which the project's own documents argue increases surface area and templates the Ag deposition. <span class="tag-assume">[ASSUMPTION]</span> The precise relationship between etch time and Ag morphology has not been characterized microscopically in this project; the R6G data shows etch time strongly affects SERS response (2&nbsp;min&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;10&nbsp;min&nbsp;&gt;&nbsp;5&nbsp;min, <strong>non-monotonic</strong>), which is documented evidence that etch time matters, but not documented evidence of exactly why.</p>

<h3>How does the biomarker interact with the sensing surface?</h3>
<p><span class="tag-assume">[ASSUMPTION, reasoned from documents]</span> Interaction mode differs by molecule, per the project's own NAM_Capture_Strategies and NAM_SERS_Engineering_Report documents:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>LPS</strong>: expected physisorption via lipid&nbsp;A's fatty-acyl chains onto bare Ag &mdash; the documented rationale for why LPS is the priority-1 target (it has a real, literature-precedented adsorption mechanism onto silver, unlike NAM).</li>
<li><strong>LTA</strong>: expected physisorption via its lipid anchor, with its negatively-charged phosphodiester backbone offering a secondary electrostatic capture route (hence the proposed cysteamine positively-charged surface as a fallback).</li>
<li><strong>NAM</strong>: <span class="tag-done">[DONE, negative finding]</span> has <em>no</em> demonstrated adsorption mechanism to bare Ag &mdash; it has no thiol, no strong Ag-binding group, and its amine is blocked as an amide. This is the documented root-cause explanation for two consecutive null SERS results (June/July batch, and the fresh 12&nbsp;Aug batch).</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>Is the sensor expected to rely on direct adsorption, selective capture chemistry, or both?</strong> <span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED framing, grounded in the documents' own staged logic]</span> Both, but sequenced: direct adsorption is tested first for every biomarker (cheapest, fastest, and the only route with any chance of near-term success for LPS/LTA); capture chemistry (4-MPBA for NAM, cysteamine as an LTA fallback) is escalated to only when direct adsorption is shown to fail. This is exactly the sequencing already written into the NAM and LTA experiment plans &mdash; this roadmap does not change that logic, it makes it explicit as a sensor-design principle.</p>

<h3>Role of 4-MPBA for NAM</h3>
<p><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED, not yet executed]</span> 4-mercaptophenylboronic acid is proposed as a bifunctional capture/reporter layer: the thiol group chemisorbs to the Ag surface (anchoring the layer where the SERS field is strongest), and the boronic-acid head group is intended to reversibly bind cis-diol-containing sugars. <span class="tag-caution">Two important documented caveats</span>: (1) NAM's own C-2 (acetamido) and C-3 (lactyl ether) substitutions block the classic 1,2-diol boronic-acid binding site, so free NAM may bind only weakly via its non-adjacent C-4/C-6 hydroxyls &mdash; the project's own design document treats this as an open, unresolved risk, not a settled mechanism; (2) 4-MPBA's own strong Raman bands (1072 and 1585&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;) sit very close to two of NAM's own bands, so the recommended NAM detection window if 4-MPBA is used is 800&ndash;1000&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; (930, 956, 871, 830&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;), avoiding the crowded region.</p>

<h3>How could LPS and LTA be detected?</h3>
<p><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span> Both via direct label-free adsorption onto bare Ag/black-Si first (no capture chemistry), following literature-precedented peak tables (LPS: 989/1330&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; primary, from Wu et al. 2021; LTA: no complete published SERS spectrum exists, so target bands are inferred from LPS's shared lipid/phosphate chemistry &mdash; 1080&ndash;1095 and 1450/1131&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;). If bare-Ag fails: LPS falls back to an alternative substrate architecture ("Sensor A/B", Part 4); LTA falls back to a cysteamine positively-charged surface.</p>

<h3>How could the three biomarkers eventually be integrated?</h3>
<p><span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED &mdash; explicitly not yet decided anywhere in the documents]</span> Three architectures are possible in principle (developed fully in Part 13): separate sensing regions per biomarker on one chip; a single surface with a blended/mixed capture chemistry; or a single bare surface relying purely on spectral deconvolution. <strong>No document in this project selects one of these</strong> &mdash; this is explicitly listed as a decision to bring to Prof. Ajay (Part 19), and only becomes relevant after at least one biomarker is individually, reproducibly detected.</p>

<h3>Physical form of the final sensor</h3>
<p><span class="tag-assume">[ASSUMPTION &mdash; not established in the documents]</span> No document specifies whether the end target is a single reusable chip, a disposable cartridge, or a multi-region multiplexed surface. The current physical form demonstrated is a single ~1&nbsp;cm&times;1&nbsp;cm silicon piece per condition, used once, not reused. Cartridge/POC packaging appears only in aspirational planning documents (Publication_Strategy, SERS_Nanobiosensor_Timeline) as a Month-14&ndash;18 goal, with no engineering design work evidenced yet.</p>

<h3>Role of the Raman reader and the software/data-analysis layer</h3>
<p><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> Reader: BWTEK i-Raman Plus (BWS465-785H spectrometer + BTC665N-785H-SYS probe), 785&nbsp;nm, benchtop, not portable. <span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> Software layer: a mature Python preprocessing pipeline exists (cosmic-ray/despike &rarr; ALS baseline &rarr; Savitzky-Golay smoothing &rarr; normalization &rarr; peak detection with SNR/pixel-width/position criteria), proven on the NAM reference dataset (227 spectra) and the R6G validation dataset (97+ spectra). <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED, not built]</span> A classification/ML layer is aspirational only &mdash; no real positive-detection training data exists yet for any biomarker.</p>

<h3>Conceptual architecture (end-to-end)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Block</th><th>What it is</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>Sample</td><td>Purified biomarker powder (NAM/LTA/LPS, Sigma-Aldrich standards) dissolved in water; eventual target is bacterial lysate/serum/blood, not yet attempted</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> for pure-standard prep protocols; <span class="tag-assume">clinical matrix not started</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Sample preparation</td><td>Weigh &rarr; dissolve &rarr; vortex &rarr; sonicate &rarr; serial dilution (protocols exist and are fairly mature for all three biomarkers)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> as SOPs; execution mostly <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Sensing/capture surface</td><td>Bare Ag/black-Si (current); + capture chemistry layer (4-MPBA / cysteamine) if bare-Ag fails</td><td>Bare surface <span class="tag-done">[DONE, fabricated and R6G-validated]</span>; capture layer <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED, not built]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>SERS enhancement</td><td>Localized plasmonic field enhancement at Ag hot spots</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> confirmed via R6G, <span class="tag-assume">not yet confirmed for any real biomarker</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Raman acquisition</td><td>785&nbsp;nm, 400&ndash;1800&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, integration/power/accumulation per protocol</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> for R6G/NAM/LTA attempts; LPS <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Preprocessing</td><td>Despike &rarr; baseline &rarr; smooth &rarr; normalize</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span>, mature pipeline</td></tr>
<tr><td>Feature extraction</td><td>Peak detection: SNR&ge;3, &ge;3-pixel width, &plusmn;5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; tolerance</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span>, formalized after the 14&nbsp;Aug pixel-artifact finding</td></tr>
<tr><td>Biomarker identification</td><td>Peak-position match to literature/DFT-predicted bands, blank/control comparison</td><td><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span> &mdash; not yet successful for any of the three biomarkers</td></tr>
<tr><td>Quantification/classification</td><td>Concentration-response curve, eventually ML classification</td><td><span class="tag-assume">[NOT STARTED]</span> &mdash; requires a successful detection first</td></tr>
<tr><td>Final result</td><td>Presence/absence + (eventually) Gram classification / concentration estimate</td><td><span class="tag-assume">[NOT STARTED]</span></td></tr>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>PART 2 — Sensor Architecture</h2>

<h3>Component status</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Layer</th><th>Description</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>A. Substrate layer</td><td>Single-crystal Si piece (n-type, per earlier lab protocols &mdash; exact doping/orientation of the specific Aug batch <span class="tag-assume">not re-confirmed in the Aug documents</span>)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>B. Nanostructured Ag layer</td><td>One-step MACE deposition, uncharacterized morphology (no FESEM)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE, functionally]</span> / <span class="tag-assume">[morphology unverified]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>C. Capture/functionalization layer</td><td>4-MPBA (NAM) or cysteamine (LTA), or two-step re-deposited Ag ("Sensor A", LPS)</td><td><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED, none built yet]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>D. Sample-loading region</td><td>Open droplet deposition, optionally confined by a hydrophobic barrier ring (wax/PAP pen)</td><td><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span> &mdash; barrier rings specified in protocols, not yet validated in a run</td></tr>
<tr><td>E. Sensing region</td><td>Same as B/C &mdash; no separate "sensing vs. structural" region has been engineered; the whole etched piece is both</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE, undifferentiated]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>F. Raman measurement region</td><td>Multiple discrete spots per piece (point measurements, not mapping)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>G. Data-analysis layer</td><td>Python preprocessing + peak-detection pipeline</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span></td></tr>
</table>

<div class="assume"><strong>Do not read the diagrams below as a finalized geometry.</strong> No document in this project specifies exact layer thicknesses, particle sizes, or a finalized cross-section. These diagrams are schematic engineering illustrations of what has been built/proposed, not an approved design drawing.</div>

