<tr><td>Reproducibility between substrates</td><td>Batch-to-batch consistency</td><td>Same condition, independent substrates</td><td>Correlation across substrates</td><td>Target corr.&gt;0.90 (stated project target, not yet achieved)</td><td><span class="tag-assume">Never tested</span> &mdash; only 1 substrate per condition made in every session to date</td></tr>
<tr><td>Reproducibility between spots</td><td>Within-substrate consistency</td><td>&ge;3 spots per condition</td><td>Spot-to-spot correlation/CV</td><td>Mean correlation&ge;0.90 target; documented actual 0.7754 (June batch)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE, below target]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Enhancement factor (EF)</td><td>Quantify absolute SERS gain vs. non-enhanced Raman</td><td>SERS intensity vs. bulk/solution reference</td><td>EF value (stated KPI target &gt;10&#8310;)</td><td>Not yet defined as a pass/fail number</td><td><span class="tag-assume">No EF calculation exists anywhere in the reviewed documents</span>, despite being a stated project KPI</td></tr>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>PART 6 — R6G Quality Control</h2>
<p><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span> R6G is used because it is a strong, resonantly-enhanced, well-characterized benchmark dye with literature-consensus peak positions (612, 774, 1183, 1310, 1362, 1509, 1575, 1650&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;) &mdash; it tells you the substrate and instrument are working, <em>before</em> you spend biomarker material finding out the hard way. It does <strong>not</strong> tell you a weak, non-resonant analyte like NAM will also be detectable &mdash; this exact point is explicitly flagged in the documents as the reason NAM failed twice despite R6G working well both times.</p>
<h3>What should be measured / tracked</h3>
<ul>
<li>SNR at each of the 8 standard bands, primarily 1509&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; (used as the go/no-go metric, threshold SNR&ge;10 <span class="tag-plan">per protocol</span>)</li>
<li>Peak position vs. literature/DFT-scaled prediction (documented: 84.58% peak-matching accuracy, mean shift +0.81&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, June batch)</li>
<li>Spot-to-spot and substrate-to-substrate reproducibility (documented actual: mean correlation 0.7754, below the stated 0.90 target)</li>
</ul>
<h3>How many spots/substrates should eventually be measured</h3>
<p><span class="tag-prop">[Proposed, consistent with existing protocols]</span> Minimum 3 spots per new substrate as an immediate gate; the protocols already specify a fuller R6G series (0.1/0.01/0.001&nbsp;mM) on the single best-performing condition once a winner is picked from a comparative batch (e.g., the AgNO&#8323; series or the Sensor A re-deposition-time series).</p>
<h3>How R6G failure should affect the experiment</h3>
<p><span class="tag-plan">[DONE, already the governing rule]</span> If R6G doesn't clear the SNR threshold, that substrate/condition is discarded as a <em>fabrication</em> problem before any biomarker material is used &mdash; it is never interpreted as evidence about the biomarker itself.</p>

<hr>

<h2>PART 7 — Sample Loading / Droplet Strategy</h2>
<h3>Documented problem</h3>
<p><span class="tag-done">[DONE, documented finding]</span> The black-Si surface is strongly hydrophilic; a 3&nbsp;&micro;L drop was observed to spread across an entire piece (12&nbsp;Aug session), carrying analyte to the perimeter (coffee-ring effect) and out of the measured spot &mdash; identified as a contributing factor (not the sole cause) to the NAM/LTA null results.</p>
<h3>Proposed strategy already in the documents</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span> Reduce drop volume to 2&ndash;3&nbsp;&micro;L (matches the literature LPS protocol exactly, 2.0&nbsp;&micro;L).</li>
<li><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span> Hydrophobic barrier rings (wax/PAP pen), 3&ndash;5 rings per substrate, ~3&ndash;4&nbsp;mm apart, dried ~5&nbsp;min before use, to physically confine the droplet.</li>
<li><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span> Vacuum-desiccator drying preferred over air-dry; air-dry acceptable only if logged as a deviation.</li>
</ul>
<h3>New engineering suggestions (this roadmap, not yet in any document)</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="tag-prop">Raman mapping instead of single-point measurement</span> within each confined drop &mdash; already suggested in passing in NAM_SERS_Sensor_Report as a way to address the coffee-ring problem directly (measure across the ring, not just the center), but never implemented in any executed session.</li>
<li><span class="tag-prop">Incubation time before drying</span> as an explicit, logged variable &mdash; not currently tracked in any protocol.</li>
<li><span class="tag-prop">Future microfluidic/sample-well integration</span> &mdash; no document proposes this yet; it is a natural longer-term step toward a cartridge-format sensor (Part 1) but is well beyond the current single-drop, single-spot measurement approach and should be treated as a Phase&nbsp;8+ concept (Part 15), not a near-term task.</li>
</ul>

<hr>

