<table>
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Objective</th><th>Experiment</th><th>Variables</th><th>Controls</th><th>Success criteria</th><th>Failure criteria</th><th>Next decision</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>1 &mdash; Bare Ag/Si optimization</strong> <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span></td><td>Test whether nanostructure/hotspot density compensates for NAM's poor adsorption</td><td>AgNO&#8323; series: 5/10/20/40/80&nbsp;mM, fixed 2-min etch, fixed NAM conc.</td><td>AgNO&#8323; concentration only (etch time, NAM conc., settings all held constant)</td><td>Same-session blank; R6G gate per group</td><td>&ge;2 primary NAM bands (930/871&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;), SNR&ge;3, &ge;2/3 spots, on any group</td><td>No group clears criteria</td><td>If success: reproducibility check, LOD. If failure: proceed to conditional NAM concentration series (5&times;/10&times;), then Stage&nbsp;2</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>2 &mdash; Capture chemistry evaluation</strong> <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED, only if Stage 1 fails]</span></td><td>Test whether 4-MPBA anchoring rescues detection</td><td>4-MPBA monolayer formation, confirmed via its own 1072/1585&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; bands, then NAM exposure</td><td>MPBA concentration/incubation time (not yet defined &mdash; <span class="tag-assume">open gap</span>)</td><td>MPBA-only spectrum recorded first (mandatory, to rule out interference); bare-Ag NAM comparison</td><td>MPBA layer stable (CV&lt;15% across substrates); then NAM bands appear in the 800&ndash;1000&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; window with MPBA present but not without</td><td>MPBA layer itself unstable, or no NAM signal even with MPBA present</td><td>If success: Stage&nbsp;3. If failure: fall back to alternative capture chemistries (hydrazide, cysteamine) per the documented ranking, or treat NAM as detectable only via bacterial capture (not free molecule)</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>3 &mdash; Optimize capture conditions</strong> <span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED, not yet detailed in any document]</span></td><td>Tune MPBA concentration/incubation/pH for maximum, reproducible NAM signal</td><td>MPBA concentration series, incubation time series</td><td>MPBA concentration, time, pH</td><td>Fixed NAM concentration throughout</td><td>Reproducible NAM signal at the ratiometric marker I(930)/I(1072)</td><td>No condition improves on Stage&nbsp;2's best result</td><td>Proceed to Stage&nbsp;4 or reconsider capture chemistry family</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>4 &mdash; Validate specificity/reproducibility</strong> <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED, staged in NAM_Detection_Experimental_Plan]</span></td><td>Confirm the signal is genuinely NAM, not glucose or another interferent</td><td>GlcNAc (closest analogue) and glucose (physiological interferent, ~5&nbsp;mM in blood) comparison</td><td>Analyte identity</td><td>NAM+glucose mixture</td><td>Discrimination via lactyl-carrying bands (1337&ndash;1385, &gt;1750&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;), though these fall in the reporter-congested region &mdash; may need a different reporter</td><td>No discrimination possible</td><td>If success: proceed to complex-matrix testing (serum/blood). If failure: reconsider reporter chemistry</td></tr>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>PART 9 — LTA Sensor Development (Corrected Strategy)</h2>
<p>This section exists specifically to prevent repeating the 14&nbsp;Aug mistake, where a real-looking SNR&nbsp;7&ndash;9 signal at 1095&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; turned out to be a single detector pixel (index 640) misbehaving identically across three consecutive spectra.</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Safeguard</th><th>What it requires</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>Fresh LTA preparation</td><td>Confirm the age of any existing stock before reuse (aqueous LTA stable only 2 days at 4&deg;C, or 2 months as frozen single-use aliquots); prepare fresh or discard if uncertain</td><td><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Same-session blank</td><td>Bare-substrate spectrum recorded immediately before LTA, same settings</td><td><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED, now standard]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Mandatory despiking</td><td>Reject any candidate feature narrower than 3 detector pixels (~5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;) before any peak is called &mdash; a genuine band on this instrument spans 4&ndash;5 pixels (established from the Si TO-phonon FWHM)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE, rule established 13&ndash;14&nbsp;Aug]</span>, <span class="tag-plan">application to next LTA run is planned</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Detector-pixel-width rule (explicit check)</td><td>Explicitly verify pixel width of every candidate peak, not just visual inspection; check whether pixel&nbsp;640 specifically is a persistent hot pixel by examining a dark/blank spectrum</td><td><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Multiple spots</td><td>&ge;9 spots at primary concentration (matches the LPS reproducibility standard adopted for LTA too)</td><td><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Reproducibility</td><td>2nd substrate repeat at the primary concentration</td><td><span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Spectral comparison</td><td>Require BOTH a phosphate band AND a lipid band before calling "detected" &mdash; a lone phosphate band is not LTA-specific (also seen in nucleic acids, LPS)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE, criterion established]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Decision criteria</td><td>Formal DETECTED / POSSIBLY DETECTED / NOT DETECTED framework (Part&nbsp;11)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE, defined]</span></td></tr>
</table>

