<h2>PART 11 — Detection Criteria (Formal Framework)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Threshold</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Spectral position</td><td>Within &plusmn;5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; of literature/DFT target (accounts for the documented &minus;2 to &minus;3&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1; calibration offset)</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. SNR</td><td>&ge;3 above local flanking-baseline noise</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Peak width / detector pixels</td><td>&ge;3 pixels (~5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;) &mdash; mandatory, from the 14&nbsp;Aug pixel-640 finding</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Spatial reproducibility</td><td>Present in a defined minimum fraction of spots (&ge;2/3 for NAM groups, &ge;3/9 for LTA, &ge;5/9 for LPS primary concentration &mdash; thresholds as set in each protocol, not yet uniform across all three)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Substrate-to-substrate reproducibility</td><td>Confirmed on a 2nd independent substrate <span class="tag-plan">specified, never yet executed for any condition</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>6. Blank comparison</td><td>Band must not appear above SNR&nbsp;3 in the same-session blank</td></tr>
<tr><td>7. Spectral similarity (multi-band)</td><td>For LTA specifically: co-occurrence of a phosphate band AND a lipid band required, not one alone</td></tr>
<tr><td>8. Statistical significance</td><td><span class="tag-assume">Not formally defined anywhere</span> beyond the SNR/reproducibility rules above &mdash; no p-value/hypothesis-test framework exists in any protocol reviewed</td></tr>
<tr><td>9. Concentration response</td><td>Peak height/area should track concentration monotonically (within error) across the dilution series &mdash; specified for LPS ("do not normalize across concentrations &mdash; raw intensity differences are the signal"), not yet formalized for NAM/LTA</td></tr>
<tr><td>10. Repeatability</td><td>Consistent result across repeat measurements at the same spot/condition</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Formal decision framework</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Verdict</th><th>Requirement</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>DETECTED</strong></td><td>All primary target band(s) present, SNR&ge;3, &ge;3 pixels wide, at the required spatial reproducibility fraction, within &plusmn;5&nbsp;cm&#8315;&sup1;, absent from the blank, and (where a multi-band rule applies, e.g. LTA) co-occurring with the required secondary band</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>POSSIBLY DETECTED</strong></td><td>Partial criteria met &mdash; e.g. one of two required bands only, or borderline SNR (2&ndash;3), or reproducibility below threshold but nonzero &mdash; must be flagged for repeat, never reported as a positive</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>NOT DETECTED</strong></td><td>No candidate band clears SNR&ge;3 with &ge;3-pixel width reproducibly, or all candidates fail the pixel-width/blank-comparison test</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>INCONCLUSIVE</strong></td><td><span class="tag-prop">[New, added by this roadmap for completeness]</span> Result depends on an unresolved data-quality issue (e.g., an unconfirmed hot-pixel location, an uncertain solution-age/degradation question, or a substrate that failed its own R6G gate) &mdash; must be re-run under corrected conditions before any DETECTED/NOT DETECTED verdict is assigned. This is exactly the category the 14&nbsp;Aug LTA result should have been placed in before being (correctly, on later analysis) reclassified.</td></tr>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>PART 12 — Validation Plan (Post-Detection)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Stage</th><th>Data required</th></tr>
<tr><td>1. Repeatability</td><td>Multiple spectra at the same spot, same session</td></tr>
<tr><td>2. Reproducibility</td><td>Multiple spots, multiple independent substrates, ideally multiple fabrication batches/days</td></tr>
<tr><td>3. Concentration response</td><td>Full dilution series with replicates at each level; monotonic trend documentation</td></tr>
<tr><td>4. Specificity</td><td>Closest-analogue and likely-interferent comparison (e.g., GlcNAc/glucose for NAM; nucleic-acid-rich control for LTA's phosphate band)</td></tr>
<tr><td>5. Interference testing</td><td>Biomarker in the presence of other panel members / matrix background</td></tr>
<tr><td>6. LOD</td><td>Lowest concentration meeting full detection criteria in the required spot fraction &mdash; project documents explicitly caution against computing a statistical 3&sigma;/slope LOD from fewer than 5 concentration points with replicates; report an "observed lowest-detected concentration" until that data exists</td></tr>
<tr><td>7. LOQ</td><td>Requires a validated quantitative (not just qualitative) response curve &mdash; not yet reachable at current data maturity</td></tr>
<tr><td>8. Robustness</td><td>Performance under small deliberate variations (drying time, minor concentration/pH shifts, different operator/day)</td></tr>
<tr><td>9. Multi-biomarker testing</td><td>All three biomarkers, matched fabrication batch, matched settings &mdash; explicitly planned as a later cross-analyte comparison once at least one shows individual signal</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="assume">None of Stages 1&ndash;9 above have data yet for any biomarker &mdash; this entire table is forward-looking, contingent on a first successful detection.</div>

<hr>

<h2>PART 13 — Multi-Biomarker Sensor (Architecture Options, Not Yet Decided)</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Architecture</th><th>Advantages</th><th>Limitations</th><th>Experimental complexity</th><th>Chemistry complexity</th><th>Data-analysis complexity</th><th>What must be demonstrated first</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>A. Separate sensing regions on one chip</strong></td><td>Each region can be independently optimized/functionalized; simplest data interpretation (spatial = identity)</td><td>Needs precise, reproducible regional functionalization; more fabrication steps</td><td>Medium-high</td><td>Medium (each region's chemistry can be simple, but must not cross-contaminate)</td><td>Low &mdash; identity is positional, not spectral</td><td>Each individual biomarker's own capture chemistry working reliably in isolation</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>B. Different functionalized spots</strong></td><td>Similar to A but more flexible/less rigidly geometric; easier to prototype with the current single-piece-per-condition workflow</td><td>Still needs each chemistry to work independently; spot-to-spot cross-talk risk during deposition</td><td>Medium</td><td>Medium</td><td>Low-medium</td><td>Same as A</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>C. Single SERS surface + spectral deconvolution/classification</strong></td><td>Simplest fabrication (one surface, one chemistry or none); most "elegant" long-term for miniaturization</td><td>Requires all three biomarkers' spectra to be simultaneously resolvable/distinguishable from a mixed spectrum &mdash; not demonstrated even for one biomarker alone yet</td><td>Low (fabrication) / very high (interpretation)</td><td>Low, if bare-Ag works for all three; high if each needs different, mutually-interfering capture chemistry (e.g., 4-MPBA's own bands already crowd NAM's spectrum)</td><td>Very high &mdash; needs a validated ML/deconvolution pipeline</td><td>Reliable individual-biomarker detection AND enough spectral separation between the three biomarkers' signatures to be distinguishable when mixed</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="key"><strong>No document in this project selects a final architecture, and this roadmap does not either.</strong> Given that not one of the three biomarkers has an individually reproducible detection yet, this decision is premature. Recommend discussing with Prof. Ajay only as a forward-looking conversation (Part&nbsp;19), not something to commit engineering effort to now.</div>

<hr>

<h2>PART 14 — AI/ML Layer (When, Not Yet)</h2>
<p><span class="tag-key">Do not start ML before reliable experimental spectra exist.</span> <span class="tag-done">[DONE, documented]</span> Zero real positive-detection spectra exist for any biomarker as of the latest documents reviewed &mdash; any classifier trained today would have nothing real to learn from.</p>
<h3>Eventual pipeline (proposed, standard structure)</h3>
<pre>
Raw spectra
   -> Preprocessing (despike, ALS baseline, Savitzky-Golay, normalize)   [DONE, pipeline exists]
   -> Feature extraction (peak position/height/area/width per band)      [DONE, exists for reference spectra]
   -> Dimensionality reduction (PCA already used for R6G condition-trend analysis)  [DONE for R6G/NAM reference data]
   -> Classification (PROPOSED options: PLS/PLSR for concentration, SVM / Random Forest / neural nets for identity)  [NOT YET APPLICABLE]
   -> Biomarker identification                                            [PENDING real detections]
   -> Concentration estimation                                            [PENDING a validated dose-response dataset]
</pre>
<h3>Methods (all labeled proposed options, not commitments)</h3>
<ul>
<li><span class="tag-prop">PCA</span> &mdash; already used successfully for exploratory analysis of the R6G and NAM reference datasets; reasonable first step once biomarker spectra exist.</li>
<li><span class="tag-prop">PLS/PLSR</span> &mdash; natural choice for concentration estimation once a validated dose-response dataset exists.</li>
<li><span class="tag-prop">SVM / Random Forest</span> &mdash; reasonable for Gram-classification once multiple biomarkers are each individually detectable.</li>
<li><span class="tag-prop">Neural networks (CNN/1D-Transformer)</span> &mdash; mentioned in aspirational planning documents (Genpact fellowship deck); requires substantially more data than this project currently has or is likely to have in the near term.</li>
</ul>
<h3>Data requirements before ML claims are meaningful</h3>
<p><span class="tag-prop">[Proposed, general good practice, not a documented project figure]</span> At minimum: multiple independent substrates &times; multiple concentrations &times; multiple replicate spots, for each biomarker, with a held-out test set never used in training. Given the current state (zero confirmed detections), this is realistically a Phase&nbsp;9 activity (Part&nbsp;15), not a near-term one.</p>

<hr>

