<tr><td>5 &mdash; Capture chemistry <span class="tag-plan">[PLANNED, conditional]</span></td><td>Rescue NAM/LTA detection via functionalization</td><td>4-MPBA (NAM), cysteamine (LTA)</td><td>Functionalized-surface spectra</td><td>MPBA/cysteamine-only + biomarker-present spectra</td><td>Signal appears with capture layer, absent without</td><td>No improvement over bare-Ag</td><td>Success &rarr; Phase&nbsp;6; failure &rarr; alternative capture chemistries or treat as a documented negative</td></tr>
<tr><td>6 &mdash; Biomarker optimization <span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED, standard next step]</span></td><td>Tune capture-layer conditions</td><td>Concentration/incubation/pH series for whichever capture chemistry succeeded</td><td>Optimized protocol</td><td>Condition-response data</td><td>Reproducible, improved signal vs. Phase&nbsp;5 baseline</td><td>No condition improves</td><td>Phase&nbsp;7</td></tr>
<tr><td>7 &mdash; Quantitative validation <span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED]</span></td><td>Move from qualitative detection to quantitative characterization</td><td>Full Part&nbsp;12 validation stages</td><td>LOD/LOQ, reproducibility statistics</td><td>Multi-substrate, multi-batch dilution series</td><td>Statistically defensible LOD from &ge;5 concentration points with replicates</td><td>Data too noisy/irreproducible to support a defensible LOD</td><td>Phase&nbsp;8</td></tr>
<tr><td>8 &mdash; Multiplexing <span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED, contingent]</span></td><td>Combine &ge;2 validated biomarkers on one platform</td><td>Architecture selection (Part&nbsp;13) with Prof. Ajay, then build</td><td>Multi-biomarker chip/protocol</td><td>&ge;2 biomarkers each individually validated (Phase&nbsp;7 complete)</td><td>Simultaneous or side-by-side detection without cross-interference</td><td>Cross-interference prevents reliable multi-biomarker readout</td><td>Phase&nbsp;9</td></tr>
<tr><td>9 &mdash; AI/ML analysis <span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED, contingent]</span></td><td>Automate classification/quantification</td><td>Build pipeline per Part&nbsp;14 once sufficient real data exists</td><td>Trained, validated classifier</td><td>Multi-substrate, multi-batch, multi-concentration dataset with held-out test set</td><td>Held-out test performance meets a criterion to be agreed with Prof. Ajay</td><td>Insufficient data diversity to support any real-world claim</td><td>Phase&nbsp;10</td></tr>
<tr><td>10 &mdash; Integrated sensor prototype <span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED, longest-term]</span></td><td>Package into a usable device concept</td><td>Physical form decision (Part&nbsp;1), possible microfluidic/cartridge integration (Part&nbsp;7)</td><td>Prototype demonstration</td><td>All prior phases validated</td><td>Working demonstration meeting TCS-defined deliverable (Part&nbsp;18/19)</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>Project completion / handoff</td></tr>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>PART 16 — Priority Order (Dependency-Based, Not Subjective)</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Absolute laser power measurement</strong> &mdash; a one-time, near-zero-cost check that every subsequent measurement's interpretation depends on; should happen before or alongside everything else, not after.</li>
<li><strong>LPS detection</strong> &mdash; must come before any multiplexing or cross-analyte comparison work, and is the best-positioned single experiment to produce the project's first real positive result, which then informs whether Sensor&nbsp;A's decoupled-Ag approach is worth applying to NAM/LTA too.</li>
<li><strong>NAM AgNO&#8323; optimization</strong> and <strong>LTA repeat</strong> &mdash; can run in parallel with each other (independent hypotheses, independent materials) once basic instrument gaps (#1) are closed; do not depend on LPS's outcome, though they can benefit from whatever substrate-architecture lesson LPS teaches.</li>
<li><strong>Substrate characterization (FESEM/EDS/UV-Vis)</strong> &mdash; logically belongs early (it would explain <em>why</em> R6G work varies non-monotonically with etch time), but has zero documented blocking dependency on the biomarker experiments themselves &mdash; can run in parallel if microscopy access allows, otherwise is a background task.</li>
<li><strong>Commercial SERS substrate control</strong> &mdash; only becomes decision-relevant if AgNO&#8323; optimization (NAM) or the LTA repeat still fail; it exists specifically to separate "our fabrication" from "the analyte is just hard," so it is naturally sequenced after Stage 1 experiments show a null, not before.</li>
<li><strong>4-MPBA functionalization</strong> &mdash; explicitly depends on Stage 1 (bare-Ag) NAM results being null; doing it earlier would make any positive result impossible to interpret (can't tell if capture chemistry or nanostructure caused it).</li>
<li><strong>Reproducibility testing (multi-substrate)</strong> &mdash; logically belongs as soon as any condition shows promise, so it should be woven into whichever experiment (LPS, NAM, or LTA) produces the first positive, not deferred to the end.</li>
<li><strong>Concentration response</strong> &mdash; depends entirely on having a detected condition to build a dilution series around; cannot precede detection.</li>
<li><strong>LOD/LOQ</strong> &mdash; depends on concentration-response data with sufficient points/replicates; explicitly the next step after #8, not before.</li>
<li><strong>Multiplexing</strong> &mdash; depends on &ge;2 biomarkers being individually validated (Phase&nbsp;7 complete for each); premature before that.</li>
<li><strong>ML</strong> &mdash; depends on a real, diverse dataset existing across multiple substrates/batches/concentrations; currently blocked by the fact that zero such datasets exist for any biomarker.</li>
</ol>

