<h2>PART 20 — Final One-Page Work Plan</h2>
<table>
<tr><td style="background-color:#1F3864;color:#fff;font-weight:bold;">PROJECT OBJECTIVE</td><td>Label-free SERS detection of sepsis biomarkers NAM (universal), LTA (Gram-positive), LPS (Gram-negative) on a chemically-fabricated Ag/black-Si substrate, 785&nbsp;nm excitation.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background-color:#1F3864;color:#fff;font-weight:bold;">TARGET SENSOR</td><td>Not finalized in any document. Working concept: bare-Ag/black-Si surface as the base platform, with biomarker-specific capture chemistry (4-MPBA for NAM, possibly cysteamine for LTA) added only where bare adsorption fails; physical form (single chip vs. multiplexed cartridge) explicitly undecided &mdash; a question for Prof. Ajay, not yet an engineering commitment.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background-color:#1F3864;color:#fff;font-weight:bold;">CURRENT STATUS</td><td>Substrate <span class="tag-done">works</span> (R6G SNR to 450). NAM tried twice, <span class="tag-done">null</span> (structural cause). LTA tried once, <span class="tag-done">corrected to null</span> (detector artifact). LPS <span class="tag-assume">never attempted</span>. Reference DFT/Raman work for all three is complete to varying quality; NAM manuscript ~85-100% ready, not submitted. No experiment run since 16&nbsp;Aug.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background-color:#1F3864;color:#fff;font-weight:bold;">NEXT EXPERIMENT</td><td>LPS detection, 2-day fast-track (Sensor&nbsp;A architecture), this week &mdash; pending HNO&#8323;/pyrogen-free-water confirmation. In parallel: measure absolute laser power in mW (one-time, unblocks interpretation of everything else).</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background-color:#1F3864;color:#fff;font-weight:bold;">SENSOR DEVELOPMENT PHASES</td><td>0&nbsp;Reference (done) &rarr; 1&nbsp;Substrate (done, partial) &rarr; 2&nbsp;LPS PoC &rarr; 3&nbsp;NAM optimization &rarr; 4&nbsp;LTA repeat &rarr; 5&nbsp;Capture chemistry (conditional) &rarr; 6&nbsp;Optimization &rarr; 7&nbsp;Quantitative validation &rarr; 8&nbsp;Multiplexing &rarr; 9&nbsp;AI/ML &rarr; 10&nbsp;Integrated prototype.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background-color:#1F3864;color:#fff;font-weight:bold;">3-MONTH TARGETS</td><td>Weeks&nbsp;1-2: LPS verdict. Weeks&nbsp;3-4: NAM AgNO&#8323; series verdict. Weeks&nbsp;5-6: LTA clean repeat verdict. Weeks&nbsp;7-9: reproducibility + capture-chemistry follow-up for whichever failed. Weeks&nbsp;10-12: concentration-response/LOD for the strongest performer, NAM manuscript submission, consolidated review.</td></tr>
<tr><td style="background-color:#1F3864;color:#fff;font-weight:bold;">FINAL EXPECTED OUTPUT</td><td>Not established in the available documents &mdash; the exact TCS deliverable has never been defined in any document reviewed. This is the single most important open question for today's discussion.</td></tr>
</table>

<hr>

<h2>PART 21 — Alternative Substrate Track: Ag/Porous-Si (Addendum)</h2>
<p><span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED, 18 Sep 2026]</span> Raised as a literature-replication track, not a speculative new substrate: reproduce a published Ag/porous-Si SERS platform, validate it on your own 785&nbsp;nm system with R6G, then compare it against the existing Ag/Black-Si platform for LPS detection. Key references: Zeiri et al. 2012 (<em>Applied Spectroscopy</em> 66, 294&ndash;299, spontaneous Ag deposition on porous Si, morphology varies with Ag&#8314; concentration); Kosovi&#263; et al. 2015 (<em>Applied Spectroscopy</em> 69, Ag immersion plating on anodized porous Si); Tsao et al. 2021 (<em>Analyst</em> 146, 7645&ndash;7652, localized hydrophilic Ag/PS sensing pads surrounded by hydrophobic porous-Si &mdash; directly relevant to this project's documented droplet-spreading problem, 12&nbsp;Aug); Ge et al. 2020 (<em>ACS Appl. Nano Mater.</em>, single-step electrochemical Ag-nanodendrite/porous-Si synthesis, kept as an advanced/later alternative, not a first experiment).</p>

<h3>Wafer specification (now confirmed)</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Parameter</th><th>Value</th><th>Status</th></tr>
<tr><td>Doping type</td><td>n-type</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE, user-confirmed 18 Sep 2026]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Orientation</td><td>(100)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Resistivity</td><td>1&ndash;10&nbsp;&Omega;&middot;cm</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Polish</td><td>Single-side polished (SSP)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span></td></tr>
<tr><td>Grade</td><td>Prime-grade CZ (Czochralski)</td><td><span class="tag-done">[DONE]</span></td></tr>
</table>
<p>This closes the wafer-specification gap flagged in Part&nbsp;4/Part&nbsp;16 for the existing Ag/Black-Si MACE process. It should now be logged in the batch records for all substrates going forward, and retroactively noted as the likely (though not independently re-verified per-batch) spec for prior Aug&nbsp;2026 substrates.</p>

<h3>Compatibility flag: n-type wafer vs. the cited anodization literature</h3>
<div class="caution"><strong>Important mismatch to resolve before copying any recipe from the cited papers.</strong> Zeiri, Kosovi&#263;, and Ge&rsquo;s porous-Si substrates were predominantly made by <strong>electrochemical anodization</strong> on <strong>p-type</strong> silicon. Anodization relies on hole availability at the Si/electrolyte interface, which p-type silicon supplies abundantly as majority carriers. Moderately-doped n-type silicon (1&ndash;10&nbsp;&Omega;&middot;cm, as confirmed for this wafer) generally lacks sufficient holes in the dark at this doping level, and standard porous-silicon practice (the Lehmann-type classification of anodization regimes) calls for <strong>backside illumination</strong> (photogenerated holes) to form controlled, uniform pores on n-type wafers in this resistivity range. Anodizing this wafer with an unmodified p-type recipe from the literature would not be scientifically justified without either illumination or re-deriving the process window for n-type. <span class="tag-assume">This is a reasoned caution based on standard porous-silicon electrochemistry, not a project-specific finding &mdash; confirm with someone experienced in porous-Si anodization before committing HF/current parameters.</span></div>

<h3>Two paths forward</h3>
<table>
<tr><th>Path</th><th>Description</th><th>New equipment needed</th><th>Fidelity to cited papers</th><th>Recommendation</th></tr>
<tr><td><strong>A. Electrochemical anodization (as in Zeiri/Kosovi&#263;/Ge)</strong></td><td>Anodize the n-type wafer with backside illumination to supply holes, then Ag immersion-plate or co-deposit</td><td>DC power supply/galvanostat, PTFE or HF-resistant anodization cell, Pt/graphite counter electrode, <strong>and</strong> a backside illumination source (IR/visible lamp) &mdash; none of this exists in any project document reviewed</td><td>High &mdash; matches the cited methods directly</td><td><span class="tag-prop">Contingent</span> &mdash; only pursue if this equipment (especially illumination-capable anodization) is confirmed available; otherwise it is a multi-week procurement/setup effort before any first substrate is even made</td></tr>
<tr><td><strong>B. MACE-based porous/high-porosity Si</strong></td><td>Extend the one-step Ag&#8314;/HF electroless etch this project already uses &mdash; tuned toward deeper, more porous morphology (longer etch time, and/or an added oxidant such as H&#8322;O&#8322;, which is well precedented for improving hole injection on n-type Si in metal-assisted etching) rather than the current coupled black-Si condition</td><td><strong>None</strong> &mdash; uses the same beaker-scale wet-chemistry process, equipment, and HF-handling SOP already in use for the existing substrate</td><td>Lower &mdash; related architecture (Ag on a porous/nanostructured Si scaffold), not an exact replication of the anodization papers</td><td><span class="tag-prop">Recommended near-term path</span> given the confirmed n-type wafer and zero new equipment requirement</td></tr>
</table>

<h3>Updated recommendation</h3>
<p><span class="tag-prop">[PROPOSED]</span> Given the confirmed n-type, 1&ndash;10&nbsp;&Omega;&middot;cm wafer, Path&nbsp;B (MACE-based, higher-porosity tuning of the existing process) is the practically feasible near-term route: it requires no new equipment or safety approvals beyond what is already in place, and can be run as a small process-matrix extension of the existing AgNO&#8323;/HF etch work (e.g., longer etch times, or added H&#8322;O&#8322; as a second oxidant) rather than a parallel fabrication program. Path&nbsp;A (true anodized porous-Si, exactly matching Zeiri/Kosovi&#263;/Ge) should be treated as a Phase&nbsp;2+ item, pursued only after confirming (with Prof. Ajay and the department) whether illumination-capable anodization equipment is realistically accessible. Either path still follows the same replication discipline already specified for this addendum: fabricate &rarr; characterize (FESEM/EDS if accessible) &rarr; R6G gate and Ag/Black-Si comparison &rarr; LPS &rarr; LTA &rarr; NAM last, per the staged campaign in the original proposal.</p>

<h3>New question for Prof. Ajay (add to Part 19 list)</h3>
<div class="caution">Does the department have an electrochemical anodization setup with backside-illumination capability suitable for n-type silicon porous-Si formation, or should the porous-Si/Ag track be pursued via a MACE-based higher-porosity variant of the existing etch process instead?</div>

